@** Installation for all users. The tools were first installed in the home
directory of one user, without administrator rights. When everything worked,
a second step installed them for every user of the machine. That step needs
\texttt{sudo}, so it was written as a script for the owner of the machine to
run, rather than performed by the assistant that wrote the rest of the
program. This part presents the script, which also documents where every tool
ended up.

<<install_all_users.sh>>=
#!/usr/bin/env bash
<<Describe what the script does>>
set -euo pipefail

<<Refuse to run without root privileges>>

<<Locate the per-user installation>>

<<Install the CUDA toolkit system-wide>>

mkdir -p "$DEST"

<<Copy the OptiX headers and the Dear ImGui sources>>

<<Install SDL3 and EnTT from their build trees>>

<<Copy OpenUSD and rewrite the paths it records>>

<<Make the dynamic loader find the libraries>>

<<Set up the environment of login shells>>

<<Report the result>>
@ The opening comment is the script's own summary of its effect. The option
[[set -euo pipefail]] makes the script stop at the first failing command, at
the first use of an undefined variable, and when any command of a pipeline
fails.

<<Describe what the script does>>=
# Step 3 of logbook.md: install the visualization tools for all users.
# Reuses what was downloaded/built in ~/.local/opt (step 1).
#
#   sudo bash install_all_users.sh
#
# Result:
#   /usr/local/cuda-13.2 (+ /usr/local/cuda symlink)   CUDA toolkit
#   /opt/viztools/{optix-9.1.0,sdl3,entt-4.0.0,imgui-1.93.0,usd-26.11}
#   /etc/profile.d/viztools.sh                          environment for login shells
#   /etc/ld.so.conf.d/viztools.conf                     runtime library paths
@
<<Refuse to run without root privileges>>=
if [[ $EUID -ne 0 ]]; then
    echo "Run with sudo: sudo bash $0" >&2
    exit 1
fi
@ Under \texttt{sudo}, the home directory is that of the invoking user, which
\texttt{sudo} records in [[SUDO_USER]]. The per-user tools live in
\lpfile{.local/opt} below it; their downloads and build trees were kept in
\lpfile{.local/opt/src}, because SDL3, EnTT and CUDA are installed again from
there.

<<Locate the per-user installation>>=
USER_HOME=$(getent passwd "${SUDO_USER:-pablofigueroa}" | cut -d: -f6)
LOCAL=$USER_HOME/.local/opt
SRC=$LOCAL/src
DEST=/opt/viztools
@ CUDA is installed with NVIDIA's own installer, as the toolkit only (the
driver was already there). The test makes the script safe to run twice.

<<Install the CUDA toolkit system-wide>>=
echo "==> CUDA 13.2 toolkit -> /usr/local/cuda-13.2"
if [[ ! -x /usr/local/cuda-13.2/bin/nvcc ]]; then
    TMPDIR=$SRC bash "$SRC/cuda_13.2.1_linux.run" --nox11 -- --silent --toolkit --no-man-page --no-drm --override
fi
@ OptiX and Dear ImGui are plain directories of headers and sources, so a
copy is all they need.

<<Copy the OptiX headers and the Dear ImGui sources>>=
echo "==> OptiX 9.1.0 headers"
rm -rf "$DEST/optix-9.1.0"
cp -r "$LOCAL/optix-9.1.0" "$DEST/optix-9.1.0"

echo "==> Dear ImGui 1.93.0 sources + backends"
rm -rf "$DEST/imgui-1.93.0"
cp -r "$LOCAL/imgui-1.93.0" "$DEST/imgui-1.93.0"
@ SDL3 and EnTT were built with CMake, which can install a finished build tree
to a new prefix. This rewrites their CMake package files with the right paths.

<<Install SDL3 and EnTT from their build trees>>=
echo "==> SDL3 3.4.18"
cmake --install "$SRC/SDL3/build" --prefix "$DEST/sdl3" > /dev/null

echo "==> EnTT 4.0.0"
cmake --install "$SRC/entt/build" --prefix "$DEST/entt-4.0.0" > /dev/null
@ OpenUSD's own build script installed it directly, so it is copied instead.
Its package file, and a few others, still name the old directory, so every
text file that mentions it is edited in place. The new tree belongs to root
and is readable by everyone.

<<Copy OpenUSD and rewrite the paths it records>>=
echo "==> OpenUSD 26.11.1"
rm -rf "$DEST/usd-26.11"
cp -r "$LOCAL/usd-26.11" "$DEST/usd-26.11"

# Point text files (cmake/pkg-config) that mention the per-user location to /opt/viztools
grep -rlI "$LOCAL" "$DEST" | xargs -r sed -i "s#$LOCAL#$DEST#g"

chown -R root:root "$DEST"
chmod -R a+rX "$DEST"
@ Programs linked against CUDA, SDL3 or OpenUSD must find these shared
libraries when they start. A file in \lpfile{/etc/ld.so.conf.d} adds the three
directories to the system's search list, and \texttt{ldconfig} rebuilds the
loader's cache.

<<Make the dynamic loader find the libraries>>=
echo "==> /etc/ld.so.conf.d/viztools.conf"
cat > /etc/ld.so.conf.d/viztools.conf <<EOF
/usr/local/cuda-13.2/lib64
$DEST/sdl3/lib
$DEST/usd-26.11/lib
EOF
ldconfig
@ Login shells read every script in \lpfile{/etc/profile.d}. Ours puts
\texttt{nvcc} and the USD command-line tools on the [[PATH]], and sets the
variables that the CMake project of the build-system part looks for. The
quoted \texttt{'EOF'} keeps the dollar signs for the shell that will read the
file later.

<<Set up the environment of login shells>>=
echo "==> /etc/profile.d/viztools.sh"
cat > /etc/profile.d/viztools.sh <<'EOF'
# Visualization tools installed for all users (see /opt/viztools)
export CUDA_HOME=/usr/local/cuda-13.2
export OptiX_INSTALL_DIR=/opt/viztools/optix-9.1.0
export IMGUI_DIR=/opt/viztools/imgui-1.93.0
export PATH=$CUDA_HOME/bin:/opt/viztools/usd-26.11/bin:$PATH
export CMAKE_PREFIX_PATH=/opt/viztools/sdl3:/opt/viztools/entt-4.0.0:/opt/viztools/usd-26.11:$CUDA_HOME${CMAKE_PREFIX_PATH:+:$CMAKE_PREFIX_PATH}
EOF
chmod 644 /etc/profile.d/viztools.sh
@
<<Report the result>>=
echo "==> Done. Verifying:"
/usr/local/cuda-13.2/bin/nvcc --version | tail -1
ls "$DEST"
